WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.056 --> 00:00:07.120
OLIVIA: Hello, Little Fox readers, and welcome back to People in the News ! Today Ben is going

2
00:00:07.121 --> 00:00:13.537
to tell you about an impressive woman—Ellen Johnson Sirleaf. Ellen is the first female president of an African nation

3
00:00:13.538 --> 00:00:20.942
and a 2011 Nobel Peace Prize winner. BEN: Thanks, Olivia. As you're about to find out, "Liberia's Iron

4
00:00:20.943 --> 00:00:27.949
Lady" has led a really interesting life! She was born in 1938 in Monrovia, Liberia. According to a family

5
00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:38.683
story, an old man came to visit when Ellen was just a few days old. The man looked

6
00:00:38.684 --> 00:00:44.021
at her and then immediately declared that she would be a great leader someday. Later, whenever Ellen made a

7
00:00:44.022 --> 00:00:53.261
mistake or got into trouble, her family would teasingly remind her about the "greatness" predicted by this early

8
00:00:53.262 --> 00:01:00.094
family visitor! Ellen's father was a politician, the first indigenous man ever to be elected to the Liberian House

9
00:01:00.095 --> 00:01:06.761
of Representatives. So Ellen grew up knowing a lot about politics—and about the deep social tensions that divided

10
00:01:06.762 --> 00:01:14.491
her country. People known as "Americo-Liberians" held most of the country's power and wealth while many natives struggled to

11
00:01:14.492 --> 00:01:19.314
get well-paying jobs. Ellen's father made sure that his children grew up proud of their indigenous roots. Ellen

12
00:01:19.315 --> 00:01:26.996
remembers her childhood as a happy time until tragedy struck: her father suffered a massive stroke and was

13
00:01:26.997 --> 00:01:31.810
disabled. Soon Ellen's mother was forced to sell baked goods to help support the family. Ellen was only 17

14
00:01:31.811 --> 00:01:37.387
when she decided to marry an older man named James Sirleaf. Before long the couple had four sons,

15
00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:43.632
and Ellen was busy with the demanding job of being a mother. Ellen enjoyed raising her children, but she

16
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:48.464
began to wish that she had gone to college. Her husband was drinking a lot and was often

17
00:01:48.465 --> 00:01:52.373
violent toward her. Ellen considered leaving him, but it was a very difficult decision. Plus, she was aware that

18
00:01:52.374 --> 00:01:56.463
Liberia was a male-dominated society with few opportunities for women. Without more education, her options were very limited.

19
00:01:56.464 --> 00:02:02.453
When James had a chance to study in the United States, Ellen decided to enroll in college too. Eventually,

20
00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:06.292
she earned degrees in accounting and economics as well as a degree in public administration from Harvard University.

21
00:02:06.293 --> 00:02:11.877
After returning to Liberia, Ellen worked for the government in the Treasury Department, and later she was named finance

22
00:02:11.878 --> 00:02:19.615
minister. But as her career flourished, her marriage continued to deteriorate, with James growing increasingly violent and possessive.

23
00:02:19.616 --> 00:02:26.852
When he pointed a gun at Ellen one day, forcing one of her young sons to protect her,

24
00:02:26.853 --> 00:02:33.996
she decided to leave him. Ellen later said her violent marriage was quite difficult, but it also made her

25
00:02:33.997 --> 00:02:40.422
stronger, and prepared her for challenges that lay ahead. The 1970s were a rough time in Liberia. As

26
00:02:40.423 --> 00:02:48.018
economic and racial tensions continued to rise, people began rioting and pressuring the government for reform. When Ellen was

27
00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:58.743
invited to give a graduation speech at the College of West Africa in 1973, she spoke frankly about

28
00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:09.071
the conditions that were dividing Liberian society. Using sharp words, she stated that the wide gap between Liberia's rich

29
00:03:09.072 --> 00:03:21.624
and poor posed a huge threat to its stability, and she challenged the government to do more to

30
00:03:21.625 --> 00:03:25.399
provide equal opportunities for all its citizens. OLIVIA: Wow, that must have been a brave thing to do. BEN:

31
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:31.373
It was brave—incredibly brave, especially since she herself worked for the government. She wrote later in her autobiography

32
00:03:31.374 --> 00:03:41.637
that this speech was a turning point in her life, the moment when she "set [her] feet upon

33
00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:48.216
a path from which there was no turning back." In 1980 things reached a crisis when soldiers stormed the

34
00:03:48.217 --> 00:03:53.444
Executive Mansion and killed the president. This sparked a violent civil war, along with two more decades of

35
00:03:53.445 --> 00:03:58.601
political turmoil. At the request of the new military leader, Ellen ran one of the nation's largest banks. But

36
00:03:58.602 --> 00:04:05.362
when she spoke out against the new government's corruption, her life was threatened, and she was forced into

37
00:04:05.363 --> 00:04:13.759
exile. Ellen spent the next 17 years working for the World Bank and the United Nations, and making valuable

38
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.590
political contacts around the world. By 2003 Ellen was back in Liberia leading a movement to end government

39
00:04:19.591 --> 00:04:25.938
corruption. Two years later she was elected president, the first woman ever to lead an African nation. One of

40
00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:32.826
Ellen's immediate priorities was to repair Liberia's economy. She worked hard to create more jobs. And using her

41
00:04:32.827 --> 00:04:42.503
political connections from around the world, she made sure that Liberia would not have to pay back the billions

42
00:04:42.504 --> 00:04:45.985
of dollars it had borrowed from other countries and organizations. Ellen also instituted special reforms for women. She

43
00:04:45.986 --> 00:04:54.572
established job training programs, a police unit to help female victims of violent crimes, and she appointed many

44
00:04:54.573 --> 00:05:00.817
female ministers to her government. In addition, Ellen impressed people with her hard work and stern, decisive leadership style.

45
00:05:00.818 --> 00:05:04.892
OLIVIA: No wonder she was nicknamed "Liberia's Iron Lady!" BEN: Yes, though some Liberians call her "Mama Ellen"

46
00:05:04.893 --> 00:05:13.958
because she's a symbol of hope and healing after years of turmoil and a violent civil war. But she

47
00:05:13.959 --> 00:05:21.053
also has critics who feel she could be doing more, especially to lower Liberia's very high unemployment rate.

48
00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:31.961
Still, Ellen has won many fans around the world. She's been the recipient of many awards, including the 2011

49
00:05:31.962 --> 00:05:37.436
Nobel Peace Prize, which she shared with two other women. They were all honored for their nonviolent struggle

50
00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:42.354
for the safety of women and women's rights. In 2011 Ellen won a second term as president of Liberia.

51
00:05:42.355 --> 00:05:49.773
OLIVIA: I guess the man who visited Ellen's parents in 1938 was right—she was destined to be a

52
00:05:49.774 --> 00:05:57.417
great leader! Thanks for that fascinating story, Ben. And thanks, Little Fox readers, for tuning in to this week's

53
00:05:57.418 --> 00:06:03.235
People in the News . We'll be back soon to tell you about another person in the news.

